        -:  176:
        -:  177:/**
        -:  178: * @defgroup hierarchical_schematics Hierarchical Schematics
        -:  179: *
        -:  180: * KiCad supports nesting schematics hierarchically to simplify the creation of complex
        -:  181: * schematics designs.  A hierarchical schematic uses hierarchical sheets (#SCH_SHEET objects)
        -:  182: * to reference a given schematic file (#SCH_SCREEN objects).  Each #SCH_SHEET corresponds to
        -:  183: * a schematic file handled by a #SCH_SCREEN object.  A #SCH_SCREEN object contains schematic
        -:  184: * drawings and has a filename to read/write its data.
        -:  185: *
        -:  186: * In simple hierarchies one #SCH_SHEET object is linked to one #SCH_SCREEN object.
        -:  187: *
        -:  188: * In complex hierarchies the a #SCH_SCREEN object shared by more than one #SCH_SHEET object.
        -:  189: * Therefore all sub-sheets can also be shared. So the same #SCH_SCREEN must handle different
        -:  190: * symbol references and unit selections depending on which sheet is currently selected, and
        -:  191: * how a given subsheet is selected. #SCH_SHEET objects share the same #SCH_SCREEN object if
        -:  192: * they have the same schematic file.
        -:  193: *
        -:  194: * In KiCad each #SCH_SYMBOL and #SCH_SHEET receives a UUID when created.  These UUIDs are
        -:  195: * chained together to form #SCH_SHEET_PATH objects that allow access of instance data in the
        -:  196: * hierarchy.  The sheet paths have the form /ROOT_SHEET_UUID/SHEET_UUID/SUB_SHEET_UUID/...
        -:  197: *
        -:  198: * For a given #SCH_SCREEN #SCH_SHEET_PATH objects must:
        -:  199: *   1) Handle all #SCH_SYMBOL references and unit instance data.
        -:  200: *   2) Handle all #SCH_SHEET page number instance data.
        -:  201: *   2) Update the currently displayed sheet #SCH_SYMBOL references and #SCH_SHEET page numbers.
        -:  202: *
        -:  203: * The class #SCH_SHEET_PATH handles paths used to access a sheet.  The class #SCH_SHEET_LIST
        -:  204: * allows one to handle the full (or partial) list of sheets and their paths in a complex
        -:  205: * hierarchy.  The class #SCH_SCREENS allows one to handle a list of #SCH_SCREEN objects. It is
        -:  206: * useful to clear or save data, but is not suitable to handle the full complex hierarchy
        -:  207: * possibilities (usable in flat and simple hierarchies).
        -:  208: */
        -:  209:
        -:  210:
        -:  211:class EDA_ITEM;
        -:  212:class SCH_SHEET;
        -:  213:class SCH_SCREEN;
        -:  214:class SCH_MARKER;
